---
description: Return iterators, not materialized lists, unless a later step needs the full set.
alwaysApply: true
---

# Return iterators

A method that produces a sequence returns an iterator and yields each item. Do not allocate a `List`, array, `Vec`, or other buffer and return that when the caller walks the items.

Use the async iterator when producing an item awaits (`IAsyncEnumerable`, async generator). Use the sync iterator when it does not (`IEnumerable`, `Iterator`, generator).

Materialize only when a later step needs every item before the first can be returned (enrichment, a count that must be known first, a sort of the whole set).

```csharp
// ❌ BAD
async Task<List<Row>> ReadRows()
{
    var rows = new List<Row>();
    while (await reader.Next())
        rows.Add(reader.Current);
    return rows;
}

// ✅ GOOD
async IAsyncEnumerable<Row> ReadRows()
{
    while (await reader.Next())
        yield return reader.Current;
}
```

```typescript
// ❌ BAD
const rows: Row[] = [];
while (await reader.next()) rows.push(reader.current);
return rows;

// ✅ GOOD
async function* readRows(): AsyncIterable<Row> {
  while (await reader.next()) yield reader.current;
}
```

```rust
// ❌ BAD — collect, then hand back the vec, when the caller only iterates
fn read_rows() -> Vec<Row> { /* ... */ }

// ✅ GOOD
fn read_rows() -> impl Iterator<Item = Row> { /* yield via iterator or stream */ }
```
